2.	Because of the confidential nature of the
Farm Credit Corporation's business dealings with individual farmers, it is not considered in the public interest to divulge the names of applicants and borrowers. However, loans have been approved in the following amounts: $8,250; $10,400; $10,000; $20,000; $13,000;	$9,000; $20,000; $11,000; $25,000;
$20,000;	$27,500; $3,000;	$14,100;	$27,500;
$12,700; $22,000 and $27,500 for a total of $280,950.
3.	7-with regard to the names of the applicants, see 2 above. Three applications were declined because, in the judgment of the corporation, the applicants would not have sufficient revenue after paying operating, maintenance and living costs to repay the loans. Two applications were declined because the applicants did not have sufficient equity in the proposed farming enterprise; and two applications were declined due to excessive debts of the applicants.

I wish to tell hon. members that it was made very clear to all Canadian producers that any terms which are made available to any one producer are available to all producers of that commodity. I know the hon. member is amazed to hear that statement, as that sort of thing never happened during the 22 years during which his party was in power. However, it happens now and it has happened under this government ever since we took over office and cleaned things up five years ago.
The hon. member says he wishes I would explain carefully to him and those on the other side exactly the terms under which people can get this extremely valuable export financing. If he wishes to know what the comparison is in the minds of businessmen
558	HOUSE OF
Export Credits Insurance Act and producers in Canada as to the satisfaction given by this government and the corporation operating under this government, and what happened five years ago, all he needs to do is to ask any five, 10, 15, 20 or 100 producers. They will all tell you straight who they think are on the side of the producers and who they think were out to block them in the old days when nobody in Ottawa seemed to pay very much attention to them.
I wish to tell the hon. member this. If he wishes to know how this corporation, or anything else which this government operates, is functioning, all he has to do is to search for the information or ask me or ask the president of the corporation, or anybody else connected with it. There are two pamphlets that are available to him. If he had only the initiative or the interest to ask for them, he could get them by going down or by asking me and I would have had it sent up, a little pamphlet called "Government Export Financing". All he had to do was to turn over a couple of pages and here he would find a heading written plainly, with black on yellow that anybody can read, "Eligibility for Financing" under which appears the following:
The guiding rules that determine the eligibility of transactions for financing are:
1.	that the extended credit terms are clearly necessary and justifiable;
2.	that the transaction gives promise of continuing export trade;
3.	that the Canadian content is of a high order, in the range of 80 per cent or better;
4.	that the transaction gives rise to significant employment and industrial benefits in Canada;
5.	that the export transaction is substantial in value;
6.	that the foreign buyer and the country of purchase have a satisfactory credit standing.
Those points are made. That also is written very clearly at page 16 of another very valuable pamphlet entitled, "Export Credits Insurance Corporation, What it is and How it Operates". It is free for the asking. It is in both of those pamphlets for anybody who wishes to ask for them. Hence, once a proposed transaction meets these requirements and it is granted to any one firm who may inquire about it, it is available to any firm or any producer in Canada who shows an interest in having those terms made available to him.
